{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Calibri;}}\f0\fs24 What a Seed Catalogue Listing Confirms Before You Request a Quote \u183? Questions and answers\par A seed catalogue page is often the first contact point for a retailer planning an order, but it is a reference tool rather than a supply commitment. Understanding what a listing establishes, and what it leaves open, helps you prepare a clear enquiry and keep stock planning realistic. The questions below address the points business buyers most often raise before requesting a quotation.\par \b Does a catalogue listing create a purchase contract?\b0\par No. A listing is a selection aid that lets business customers identify varieties and request a quotation. Adding items to a basket or submitting an enquiry form does not conclude a purchase. An automated acknowledgement confirms only that your request has been received for review, so nothing about stock, price or delivery has been agreed at that point.\par \b What does a listing actually confirm?\b0\par It confirms three things: that a catalogue reference exists within the published range for business customers, that the entry carries identifying details such as species, cultivar qualifiers and any stated SKU, and that a price basis may be shown. Some entries use weight, others a seed count, so note the comparable unit before you plan quantities. These details are enough to build a shortlist and frame a precise enquiry.\par \b Which details are settled only at quotation stage?\b0\par Availability and quantity for your requested items, pack sizes, final prices and applicable taxes, shipping charges and delivery arrangements, and payment terms all belong to the quotation. Listed prices relate to catalogue entries and do not represent current stock. A requested delivery date is likewise a request, not a confirmed commitment, so plan your own timelines around the agreed arrangements rather than the enquiry.\par \b Can catalogue wording be used as proof of resistance traits?\b0\par Catalogue text, including category wording, does not establish a variety's resistance traits or the test results of a particular lot. If you need documentation for a proposed shipment, ask for it for the exact lot and destination instead of relying on a page description. This distinction matters when your customers or your own quality process expect verifiable information.\par \b Why does destination change the answer?\b0\par A listing is not confirmation that a variety can be supplied to every destination. Import conditions and the responsibilities for documents, customs clearance, duties and taxes need to be clarified before a shipment is agreed. Include the destination country and postal code in your enquiry so this can be reviewed, and state which documents your business requires for that market.\par \b What should the pre-quotation checklist cover?\b0\par Quote the variety and any qualifiers exactly as listed, including colour names and F1. State quantities and units and check them before submitting. Note the price basis shown without treating it as final, name the destination country and postal code, list the documents you require, and give a requested delivery date in the knowledge that it is a request. Two catalogue figures are comparable only when they share a unit, and even then the figure is not a landed cost, since shipping, taxes and documentation charges are confirmed separately. Treating the catalogue as the opening of a review keeps your purchasing plan grounded.\par {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://bulkseedsinternational-en.blogspot.com/2026/10/what-seed-catalogue-listing-confirms.html"}{\fldrslt What a Seed Catalogue Listing Confirms Before You Request a Quote}}\par {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://bulk-seeds-international.github.io/bulkseedsinternational-en/dateien/minimax-415d569b8b5f9ba703191871/inhalt.odt"}{\fldrslt Related document}}\par }